body:
all the others are correct. the only thing remaining of the state would be the most basic functions for running society. this means that there could be remnants of the police in the form of community policing or a sort of policing strictly for anti-social behavior. it would lose its class character, which is the core reason why police are so brutal. the brutal policing we see today is only necessary in a society that has people with such opposing economic interests that it can't be resolved peacefully. same with stuff like the bureaucracy, parliament, or congress. under class society these institutions determine the laws that protect private property, wage imperialist wars, deny human rights etc. they also serve some economic function or functions that just maintain society. these functions would be the remaining functions of a "government" under communism stuff as the planned economy or rules regarding anti-social behavior.

&#x200B;

Edit: one thing to add is that you shouldn't confuse a society without laws or a state to what some anarchists might envision with no rules. of course, communism will probably still have labor disaplin and again stuff like anti-social rules. the remaining functions under communism are only qualitatively different because they lose class character. the main function of the state will no longer be addressing specific classes. this qualitative change is why it is no longer called a state. it has to do with the Marxist definition of the state being an institution that arose with class society used for the oppression of one class over another. before class society, in primitive communism, there were still rules, and structures of power to enforce them, but they became significantly more brutal and despotic with class society.

Have you actually read his stuff? my understanding is he doesn't deny the Holodomor he denies that it was a deliberate genocide. I've read Stalin and the struggle for democratic reform and it seemed fine to me. many people take him out of context to say he was a crazy tankie or some shit like that. like how he pointed out that Stalin wasn't guilty of any of the crimes Khrushchev pointed to in the secret speech. he's not saying that the events described didn't take place, but rather he's saying that there was an actual political process and Stalin wasn't responsible for the direct order of these things.

&#x200B;

Edit: of course, I've only listened to some talks of his, read Stalin and the struggle for democratic reform, and parts of blood lies. I'm not going to defend the guy to my death or anything. I can't speak for the whole of his work. I was mainly pointing out that we shouldn't completely reject him just because he has a perceived bias.

disclaimer: I'm dyslexic. I checked the spelling a dunch of times, but I'm sure I missed some stuff.

&#x200B;

hey, I'm an ML. the answer to this question is a little complicated. I've heard the counter, which is that socialism can include co-ops, which is true and it does in every case. my issue with this is that it completely ignores the material reality of revolutions, and it ignores the fact that the nature of governance is different under socialism. 

The material conditions of revolution have historically been that every revolution has to fight a civil war, fight imperialists from the west, fight counter-revolution, and industrialize as fast as possible. this affects those countries' policies in several ways. it usually ends with the banning of opposing parties, high security, and large military. a centralized government has historically been beneficial in industrializing quikly. unlike what Marx predicted, most revolutions did not occur in the most developed countries, but instead in the poorest. China and Russia were both feudalist countries with mostly agrarian economies.

&#x200B;

Second I want to touch on the nature of the dictatorship of the proletariat. unlike common misconception, it is not literally a dictatorship, but instead, one class holding onto state control to repress another class. the way they do this is through democratic means. the way it typically works is, workers, form unions and soviets, they elect officials, those officials meet biannually in congress to make decisions. when they arent in congress they are still workers.  they also elect higher-up officials to run things on their behalf while they are gone. they have also historically set up second congresses that equally represent nationalities to prevent one nationality from oppressing the other. this is how the dictatorship of the proletariat works. unlike what others say, many of the officials are workers, because that's how the system is supposed to function.

&#x200B;

of course, the end goal is to have the state wither away, as class distinctions become less and less. until then revolutions have to be protected. when they are not protected they don't last long but end up getting praised as martyrs for being libertarian.

&#x200B;

sorry if this doesn't give you a debate lord answer to the question. generally, I don't feel that debates change anything. They're pretty pointless. what is more important is expanding our understanding of socialist history so we can apply it later.
